-- 124: SUPERGUN scoping after Gate 4 (seed 123, ENGINE lane, Carnival / stage 3). Follows seeds 117, 119, 120, 122.
-- Source doc: mvc-live-skins-quarters/docs/SUPERGUN-NETCODE.md s10 (appended 2026-09-04).
-- Method step 4 (tag and store). Lane numbering: SUPERGUN NETCODE takes EVEN numbers from 120 up.
-- NOTHING IN THE s9 DESIGN CHANGES. This seed records (a) the precise scope of what the second stage established,
-- (b) that N1c is NOT closed and a cheaper protocol to close it, (c) that the s9.1 eligibility correction applies to
-- the Gate 4 cost figures so they are not yet quotable per event, and (d) a render-side blind spot that would be easy
-- to inherit silently into the SUPERGUN desync criterion.
-- Apply with tools/re_kb/apply_seed.py after the README export backup
-- (re_kb_data/_exports/re_kb_20260904-155143_pre124.surql). Idempotent and order-independent. No apostrophes in literals.
-- Do NOT re-run 07_dedup_edges.surql afterwards.
USE NS re DB kb;

UPSERT source:supergun_gate4_scoping_20260904 SET kind='analysis', strength='reproduction', ref='mvc-live-skins-quarters/docs/SUPERGUN-NETCODE.md s10; input is the ENGINE lane Gate 4 (seed 123) on Carnival / stage 3, plus RECEIPT-RUNNER-GATE-N1 (seed 121) on stage 9', note='Scoping and falsification-criteria pass over another lane result. Contains no new measurement of its own; its contribution is the boundary between what the second stage proved and what it did not, and an arithmetic check showing the Gate 4 cost figures carry the same eligibility weighting corrected in seed 122.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_saveset_stage_dependent SET status='confirmed', text='CONFIRMED by Gate 4 (seed 123): the rollback save-set REQUIREMENT is STAGE-DEPENDENT. On stage 9 the shipped blk-only set FAILS from depth 2 up; on Carnival (stage 3) the same blk-only set PASSES at every depth. The set rr (blk + GGPO counter + ctx+0x1F8230..0x1F8238) passes on both. So the shipped MvC2 save set is not wrong everywhere -- it is wrong SOMETIMES, as a function of stage, camera and situation, which is precisely the desync class that appears only online, under load, on some stages, and never in a smoke test. This vindicates the seed-122 decision to ship a wider set than the proven minimum: a quantity now OBSERVED to vary between the empty set on Carnival and eight bytes on stage 9 can vary again on a third stage, so a minimum measured from one frame of one stage was never a safe thing to ship.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_gate4_does_not_close_n1c SET status='confirmed', text='SCOPE LIMIT on Gate 4, recorded so it cannot be read as more than it is. Gate 4 does NOT show the eight bytes at ctx+0x1F8230..0x1F8238 are insufficient anywhere: the rr set passed at every depth on BOTH stages. And Gate 4 does NOT close N1c, because nobody has re-run the ctx LADDER -- the bisection of RECEIPT-RUNNER-GATE-N1 s3 -- on any stage other than 9; Gate 4 ran the blk and rr ARMS only. The SUPPORT of the requirement across stages is therefore still unmeasured, and whether the shipped 64 KB window ctx[0x1F0000..0x200000) is doing real work or is pure insurance remains UNKNOWN. Gate 4 raised the prior that stage dependence is real; it did not exhibit a single required byte outside the eight. Do not record N1c as resolved.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_gate_n1c_needed SET status='open', text='GATE N1c, RE-SCOPED 2026-09-04 after Gate 4 (supersedes the seed-122 wording, which asked for a full per-stage ladder). The question the design needs answered is not what the minimum is on stage X but whether the SHIPPED WINDOW is sufficient on stage X, and that is a THREE-ARM CHECK AT A SINGLE DEPTH rather than a bisection. Per stage, at depth 8: arm A = blk + counter (FAIL means this stage needs ctx state at all, like stage 9; PASS means it is like Carnival); arm B = plus ctx[0x1F8230..0x1F8238) the proven minimum (FAIL is the headline result N1c is looking for -- it would mean the minimum is stage-dependent in its CONTENT and not merely in its necessity); arm C = plus ctx[0x1F0000..0x200000) the SHIPPED window (FAIL FALSIFIES the SUPERGUN save set: the window must widen, or the save set becomes the whole 4 MB ctx and the speculation cap drops to about 8). Escalate to the full ladder ONLY on a stage where B fails and C passes, which is the only case where knowing the new minimum is informative. This makes N1c three short runs per stage instead of a bisection and it tests the thing the design actually rests on. FALSIFICATION, stated so it cannot be fudged: the SUPERGUN save set survives if and only if arm C passes on every stage tested; ONE arm-C failure anywhere kills the deep-speculation thesis in its current form.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_gate4_cost_not_yet_quotable SET status='open', text='The Gate 4 Carnival cost figures with set rr (depth 8 = 0.405 ms/frame, depth 120 = 1.823 ms/frame) are the SAME amortised, eligibility-weighted column that seed 122 corrected for stage 9, so they are not per-frame worst case and are not comparable to the per-event figures the SUPERGUN design budgets against. Inverting the eligibility model gives an estimate only, and it is uncomfortably sensitive to the Carnival tick count, which was not reported: assuming 200 ticks and stage-9 save costs, depth 8 is about 0.42 ms per event (about 2.5 percent of budget, about 66 percent MORE than stage 9 at 0.255) and depth 120 is about 4.5 ms per event (about 27 percent, about 14 percent LESS than stage 9 at 5.230); but at 300 ticks the depth-120 figure reads about 3.02 ms instead of about 4.52, a 50 percent spread, and it is undefined for any run of 120 ticks or fewer. THEREFORE NO CARNIVAL PER-EVENT COST IS QUOTED AS FACT. What is needed to quote one: the save, restore and resim p50 columns AND the tick count, per stage, exactly as GATE-N1 s1.2 gave for stage 9. What survives the uncertainty is the load-bearing part: per-stage cost varies IN BOTH DIRECTIONS (Carnival is more expensive than stage 9 at depth 8 and less expensive at depth 120), so per-frame rollback cost is a function of stage content rather than a constant of the engine, and a single-stage cost table cannot size a speculation cap.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_gate_n1b_needed SET status='open', text='GATE N1b, RESTATED 2026-09-04 with the Gate 4 finding folded in (supersedes the seed-122 wording). Required of the ENGINE lane: p99 and max -- not p50 -- for save, restore and resim at every depth, PER STAGE, plus the count of rollback events whose total exceeded 16.667 ms, plus the TICK COUNT of each run (needed to invert the eligibility weighting). A frame budget is a deadline, not an average, and the whole cost table is currently p50 on one stage. This lane measured tick p99/p50 ratios of 2.2x idle and 4.0x on real inputs; at depth 120 that projects a p99 rollback of 11 to 21 ms, which would miss the frame outright. Gate 4 makes this MORE load-bearing, not less: p50 on a single stage was already a weak basis for the cap, and p50 on stages whose cost varies non-monotonically with depth is weaker still. Until this lands, finding:supergun_speculation_cap_specified stays INFERRED and the default active cap of 32 stays provisional. This is not a formality -- the cap is the one design parameter that can silently cause dropped frames.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_desync_hash_is_not_pixel_agreement SET status='confirmed', text='PINNED so it cannot be inherited silently. Gate 4 records that ctx_out compared EQUAL while hypothesis H1 was in fact FALSE, because the geometry actually submitted lands in a STAGING BUFFER THE RUNNER NEVER DUMPS. Therefore ctx equality is not evidence of render-path equality, and anything gated on ctx equality inherits that blind spot. The SUPERGUN desync rule (seed 122, finding:supergun_desync_hash_rule) is not broken by this -- it hashes the SAVE SET only, which is simulation state -- but the way it could be READ is dangerous, so: THE SUPERGUN DESYNC HASH PROVES SIM AGREEMENT, IT DOES NOT PROVE PIXEL AGREEMENT, AND CTX EQUALITY IS SPECIFICALLY NOT A PROXY FOR PIXEL AGREEMENT. Two peers can hash-match on every frame and still render differently. Pixel agreement is the verification-harness owner gate and stays that way. NO SUPERGUN RESULT MAY BE REPORTED AS FRAMES MATCH ON THE STRENGTH OF A SAVE-SET HASH. This is the same class as the known project trap of offline geometry standing in for live pixels, arriving from a new direction.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_static_geometry_consequences SET status='confirmed', text='Gate 4 falsified H1: 14 of 16 perturbed geometry objects are STATIC -- read every frame, never rewritten -- and none are fully regenerated; the sim is unaffected (61/61 blk identical) but their prior content is read for PIXELS every frame. Two consequences for the SUPERGUN design, pointing in opposite directions. (1) NO SAVE-SET IMPACT, and the reasoning is stated rather than the conclusion assumed: an object never written during a match cannot differ between the straight-line and re-simulated timelines, therefore it is not rollback state; seed 122 finding:supergun_save_set_specified is unchanged. (2) DIRECT IMPACT ON RESYNC (finding:supergun_resync_unproven, Gate N5b): a resync by state transfer sends the SAVE SET, which by construction does NOT contain these static geometry objects, so a receiving peer must already hold them, loaded identically from the arc. That is fine for a same-build same-content peer but it is ANOTHER reason N5b is not a simple 277 KB transfer, alongside the 557 asset-image pointers. Separately, Gate 4 upgraded classes E, L and X from measured to PROVEN unread (about 6.9 MB of deliberate difference, 300/300 byte-identical blk, RNG identical at tick 300), which independently reinforces the Gate N1 result that DC-RAM needs no rollback: no design change, it removes a residual worry rather than adding a constraint.';

UPSERT finding:supergun_resync_unproven SET status='open', text='OPEN. Beyond the 64-frame ring, rollback cannot recover and the session must resync from a state snapshot. The state is 277,288 B, roughly 22 ms of wire on a 100 Mbit link, so unlike GGPO which can only freeze, we could in principle just resend it. DESIGNED, NOT PROVEN, and there are now TWO specific reasons to doubt it. (1) Per rr-ggpo-determinism a BATTLE-state blk is pointer-rich (557 pointers into the decompressed per-character asset image) and restoring one into a different process KILLED THE GAME TWICE; only character-select states were shown portable. (2) ADDED 2026-09-04 from Gate 4: 14 of 16 geometry objects are STATIC and read for pixels every frame, and they are NOT in the save set, so a receiving peer must already hold them loaded identically from the arc -- the transfer alone does not reconstitute a renderable peer. Same-build same-content peers make both tractable, and the receipt runner Delta = 0 arena placement removes relocation entirely when both sides map at the same base, but none of that is measured. Until Gate N5b passes, the honest fallback beyond 64 frames is a STALL, not a resync.';

-- edges
RELATE finding:supergun_saveset_stage_dependent->cites->source:supergun_gate4_scoping_20260904 SET id=cites:supergun_saveset_stage_dependent_src;
RELATE finding:supergun_gate4_does_not_close_n1c->cites->source:supergun_gate4_scoping_20260904 SET id=cites:supergun_gate4_does_not_close_n1c_src;
RELATE finding:supergun_gate4_cost_not_yet_quotable->cites->source:supergun_gate4_scoping_20260904 SET id=cites:supergun_gate4_cost_not_yet_quotable_src;
RELATE finding:supergun_desync_hash_is_not_pixel_agreement->cites->source:supergun_gate4_scoping_20260904 SET id=cites:supergun_desync_hash_is_not_pixel_src;
RELATE finding:supergun_static_geometry_consequences->cites->source:supergun_gate4_scoping_20260904 SET id=cites:supergun_static_geometry_consequences_src;
RELATE finding:supergun_gate_n1b_needed->cites->source:supergun_gate4_scoping_20260904 SET id=cites:supergun_gate_n1b_needed_src124;
RELATE finding:supergun_gate_n1c_needed->cites->source:supergun_gate4_scoping_20260904 SET id=cites:supergun_gate_n1c_needed_src124;
RELATE finding:supergun_resync_unproven->cites->source:supergun_gate4_scoping_20260904 SET id=cites:supergun_resync_unproven_src124;

-- the save-set decision now has evidence behind it, not only judgement; annotate rather than restate
UPDATE finding:supergun_save_set_specified SET note='REINFORCED 2026-09-04 by Gate 4 (seed 123) and scoped by seed 124: the save-set REQUIREMENT is CONFIRMED stage-dependent (blk-only FAILS on stage 9 and PASSES on Carnival), so shipping the minimum measured from one frame of one stage would have shipped a set now proven not to be stage-invariant. BUT the value of the 64 KB window itself is still UNQUANTIFIED: no stage has yet needed a byte outside ctx+0x1F8230..0x1F8238, and the ctx ladder has never been re-run off stage 9. See finding:supergun_gate4_does_not_close_n1c and the re-scoped Gate N1c.';
